---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

const memoryLayers = [
  {
    number: "01",
    name: "Visible trace",
    text: "One representative colour: the photograph reduced to finite digital material.",
  },
  {
    number: "02",
    name: "Source trace",
    text: "An optional compressed preview, no larger than 192 pixels on its longest edge.",
  },
  {
    number: "03",
    name: "Descriptive memory",
    text: "Machine-generated language—including its uncertainty and occasional mistakes.",
  },
  {
    number: "04",
    name: "Situated memory",
    text: "Date, place, provenance and the artist’s own note, kept distinct from machine interpretation.",
  },
  {
    number: "05",
    name: "Acquired memory",
    text: "Concepts encountered through selected texts and PDFs after the ink was created.",
  },
  {
    number: "06",
    name: "Social memory",
    text: "Relationships with other inks—including how a connection became active, dormant or historical.",
  },
  {
    number: "07",
    name: "Invented memory",
    text: "An AI reconstruction: explicitly an invention, never presented as recovery of the source.",
  },
];
---

<main class="ink-page">
  <section class="ink-hero" aria-labelledby="ink-title">
    <div class="ink-hero-film" aria-hidden="true">
      <video data-ink-hero-video data-src="/videos/digital-ink-116-memory.m4v" muted loop playsinline preload="none" tabindex="-1"></video>
    </div>
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <p class="ink-kicker">An ongoing artwork by Q888</p>
      <h1 id="ink-title">DIGITAL INK</h1>
      <p class="ink-hero-line">Images become materials with a past.</p>

      <div class="ink-hero-body">
        <div class="ink-prose ink-hero-copy">
          <p>Start with an image that means something to you. A photograph, a drawing, something you found or made. In Digital Ink, it can continue in another form: as a colour you can draw with, carrying traces of where it came from.</p>
          <p>I call this material a <strong>Smart Ink</strong>. There is a finite amount of it, and a history that keeps developing as you use it, educate it and bring it into contact with other Inks.</p>
          <p>Digital Ink is an experimental iPad drawing app, and also an art world I am building inside it. I wanted digital material to feel less disposable — more like something we might care for.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="ink-studio">
          <Image src="/images/DigitalInk_Ipad_kn.webp" alt="A drawing made in Digital Ink, on an iPad in the artist’s studio, with two Q888 cat figures beside it" width={2747} height={1538} loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" />
          <figcaption>Digital Ink on my studio iPad. The drawing is made with finite Smart Inks.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="ink-doorway">The artwork is made from pixels, but the pixels are no longer empty.</p>
      <div class="ink-actions">
        <a class="ink-button ink-button-primary" href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/digital-ink/id6757157376" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Try Digital Ink on iPad ↗</a>
        <a class="ink-button" href="#how-it-lives">Follow an Ink ↓</a>
      </div>
      <div class="ink-film-tools" data-ink-film-tools hidden>
        <p>The moving background is an AI-generated artistic imagining of this world.</p>
        <button class="ink-film-toggle" data-ink-film-toggle type="button">Pause background</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ink-section ink-idea" id="how-it-lives" aria-labelledby="idea-title">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <header class="ink-story-heading"><h2 id="idea-title">Giving a colour somewhere to come from</h2></header>
      <div class="ink-prose">
        <p>When I choose a colour in ordinary drawing software, I can use as much of it as I like. It has no particular origin, and the next time I choose it, nothing needs to have changed. Digital Ink began with a curiosity about what that freedom leaves out.</p>
        <p>What if choosing a material also meant choosing a past? An image becomes one Smart Ink, with its own colour, origin and developing biography. Drawing spends some of its finite supply; erasing can return material. Each placed pixel remains connected to the Ink it came from, so using it is also a small event in its history.</p>
        <p>The finite supply gives a gesture weight, but the idea goes beyond scarcity. I am interested in qualities that digital material can have on its own terms: learning something after it was created, carrying several kinds of memory, or developing relationships that continue beyond a single picture.</p>
        <p>An Ink can appear in different moments of its life without losing the record of how it arrived there. That continuity is what lets a colour become something you can get to know.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="ink-journey" aria-label="From an image to an artwork">
        <li><span class="journey-mark journey-image" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Image</span></li>
        <li><span class="journey-mark journey-ink" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Smart Ink</span></li>
        <li><span class="journey-mark journey-home" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>InkHome</span></li>
        <li><span class="journey-mark journey-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Canvas</span></li>
        <li><span class="journey-mark journey-art" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Artwork</span></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="ink-biography">
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>The Ink below began with an Afterlife Contract from another part of my practice. In its biography, a machine description sits beside my own note and the concepts it has acquired. They are different traces of an encounter, each with its own limits.</p>
          <p>A description can misread the image. A personal note can remember something the image never showed. Keeping those traces distinct matters to me: an Ink’s past can grow without being rewritten into one convenient story.</p>
        </div>
        <figure
        class="art-study art-study--memory"
        id="memory-study"
        data-art-study="memory"
      >
        <div class="art-study-shell">
          <div class="art-study-toolbar">
            <figcaption>
              <span>One Ink · a biography from the studio</span>
              <p>An Afterlife Contract, remembered as colour, description and learned concepts.</p>
              <q
                >What I remember is not what I was. It is what happened to me.</q
              >
            </figcaption>
            <span
              class="art-study-scroll-cue"
              data-study-cue
              data-resting="DESCEND THROUGH MY MEMORY ↓"
              >DESCEND THROUGH MY MEMORY ↓</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="art-study-frame art-study-frame--memory">
            <img
              class="art-study-base"
              data-study-base
              src="/images/memory.webp"
              alt="Digital Ink Smart Ink biography showing machine description, personal memory and learned concepts"
              width="2745"
              height="1492"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <img
              class="art-study-overlay"
              data-study-overlay
              aria-hidden="true"
              src="/images/memory_overlay.webp"
              alt="Artistic memory interpretation joining a Smart Ink biography to fragments of an Afterlife Contract and hand-drawn marks"
              width="2745"
              height="1669"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <p class="art-study-fog" aria-hidden="true">
              I REMEMBER …<br />being many pixels…
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
        <p class="ink-media-note">An artist’s study from development. As it crosses the centre of the screen, the interface gives way to a drawing of its memory.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ink-section ink-spaces" aria-labelledby="spaces-title">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <header class="ink-story-heading"><h2 id="spaces-title">Following an Ink through the world</h2></header>
      <div class="ink-prose"><p>There are three spaces in Digital Ink. Moving between them changes the part you play: meeting materials, making a picture with them, then letting someone else look at what has happened.</p></div>
      <article class="ink-space" aria-labelledby="home-title">
        <h3 id="home-title">InkHome</h3>
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>Before an Ink becomes part of a drawing, it has somewhere to exist. In InkHome you can spend time with its biography, give it texts to learn from, and see how it connects with other Inks.</p>
          <p>Some connections come easily; others remain distant or change over time. I want those differences to have room. The Inks’ relationships give them a life within the system that extends beyond being useful to my next picture.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="ink-space" aria-labelledby="canvas-title">
        <h3 id="canvas-title">Canvas</h3>
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>On the Canvas, you become the artist. You choose which Inks to bring together and where their pixels will go. This is the part where you can simply enjoy making an image.</p>
          <p>At the same time, the materials arrive with histories of their own. A drawing is a temporary meeting between them: something you compose, made from things that were already becoming something before you placed them here.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <figure class="art-study" id="canvas-study" data-art-study="canvas">
        <div class="art-study-shell">
          <div class="art-study-toolbar">
            <figcaption>
              <span>The Canvas · an artist’s interface study</span>
              <p>The application record, with another drawing growing around it.</p>
              <q>I have 174 pieces left. I am not a colour picker.</q>
            </figcaption>
            <span
              class="art-study-scroll-cue"
              data-study-cue
              data-resting="BRING ME TO THE CENTRE ↓"
              >BRING ME TO THE CENTRE ↓</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="art-study-frame art-study-frame--canvas">
            <img
              class="art-study-base"
              data-study-base
              src="/images/screen.webp"
              alt="Digital Ink canvas interface dated Tuesday 13 January at 17:23, containing a finite pixel artwork"
              width="2422"
              height="1535"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <img
              class="art-study-overlay"
              data-study-overlay
              aria-hidden="true"
              src="/images/screen_overlay.webp"
              alt="Artistic interpretation of the Digital Ink canvas, with hand-drawn roots, creatures and emotional ink symbols growing from the interface"
              width="2422"
              height="1535"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <p class="ink-media-note">The interface and its artistic interpretation belong to the same development study. The drawn roots are my way of making the materials’ connections visible.</p>
      <article class="ink-space ink-space-gallery" aria-labelledby="gallery-title">
        <h3 id="gallery-title">Gallery</h3>
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>When you finish a work, it enters the Gallery and the image becomes quiet again. You can look into the Inks that made it and follow their histories, or simply spend time with the picture.</p>
          <p>Here, interpretation belongs to the person looking. The system can show how a work came together; what it means remains open.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ink-section ink-independence" aria-labelledby="independence-title">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <header class="ink-story-heading"><h2 id="independence-title">Making room for things I do not control</h2></header>
      <div class="ink-prose">
        <p>Building the app gives me a particular kind of power: I can decide the rules of the world as well as make work inside it. That is also why I keep asking how much of the world should exist only to please its artist.</p>
        <p>I can choose an Ink, educate it, and arrange its pixels. But I want a relationship to be more than a reward for doing that correctly. Learning a concept need not make an Ink more obedient, and an Ink that contributes little to an encounter can still belong here.</p>
        <p>Sometimes an Ink contributes strongly. Sometimes it stays quiet. Leaving that possibility open changes collaboration: I have to pay attention to what is there, rather than assume every material will become exactly what I ask of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ink-continuity">
        <h3>What I mean by memory</h3>
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>Words like <em>memory</em>, <em>education</em> and <em>relationship</em> help me describe the continuity I am trying to build. They name artistic and computational structures; the Inks are not presented as conscious beings.</p>
          <p>The question is what a material can carry from one encounter into another. A source image, a learned concept, a connection that has become distant: these can change how you understand the colour in front of you, even when its visible appearance stays the same.</p>
          <p>There is room for imagination here, too, but it has to keep its own name. A reconstructed image is a new invention, not a recovered photograph. I want the world to be expressive while remaining honest about what it knows, remembers and makes up.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ink-section ink-growing" aria-labelledby="growing-title">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <header class="ink-story-heading"><h2 id="growing-title">Letting the work continue</h2></header>
      <div class="ink-prose">
        <p>Digital Ink is both an artwork and a place where further artworks can happen. I can add another experiment without replacing the world underneath it. The Inks, and the questions that brought them here, continue.</p>
      </div>
      <article class="ink-canvas-voice" aria-labelledby="voice-title">
        <div class="voice-heading"><h3 id="voice-title">Canvas Voice</h3><p class="ink-development-status">In development for a future version</p></div>
        <div class="ink-prose">
          <p>One experiment I am developing asks what might happen if the Inks gathered in a drawing could find a small voice together. Imagine asking the finished image a question, and hearing something shaped by this particular meeting of materials.</p>
          <p>The planned exchange is just five words in and five words back. An Ink could offer a meaningful signal or remain quiet. I am interested in how little might be needed for a moment of expression, and in allowing the group to have something of its own to contribute.</p>
          <p>The voice would belong to the encounter. When it ends, the temporary conversation would disappear without becoming the Inks’ memory. A later meeting could be different.</p>
          <p>Privacy is part of the artistic form here: a conversation can matter without being kept forever. Canvas Voice asks whether an artwork can briefly speak while remaining open to the next person, rather than settling into a permanent personality.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ink-text-link" href="#voice-research">The planned boundaries of Canvas Voice ↓</a>
      </article>
      <div class="ink-purpose ink-prose">
        <p>You can use Digital Ink simply to make pictures. You can also stay with the questions inside the material: who authors a work, what a colour might carry, how histories are kept, and what collaboration becomes when command and response are only part of it.</p>
        <p>For me, the app is a place to work through those questions by making things. What happens next is still part of the experiment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ink-section ink-technical" aria-labelledby="record-title">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <!-- CHOICE: the system remains inspectable without interrupting the first encounter with the artwork. -->
      <details class="ink-record" id="technical-record">
        <summary>
          <span><span class="ink-kicker">05 · OPEN SYSTEM NOTES</span><h2 id="record-title">TECHNICAL RECORD</h2><span class="record-invitation">For those who want to look underneath the artwork.</span></span>
          <span class="record-toggle" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="record-content">
          <p class="ink-section-intro">Digital Ink is a native iPad application built in Swift / SwiftUI. Smart Inks and artworks are primarily stored locally on the device. Online AI operations are optional and permission-based.</p>
          <nav class="record-nav" aria-label="Technical record sections">
            <a href="#app-architecture">Architecture</a><a href="#smart-ink-model">Smart Inks</a><a href="#ink-rules">Scarcity</a><a href="#ink-education">Education</a><a href="#ink-relationships">Relationships</a><a href="#ink-provenance">Provenance</a><a href="#ink-privacy">Privacy &amp; AI</a><a href="#voice-research">Canvas Voice</a><a href="#archive-title">116-Pixel Archive</a><a href="#development-status">Development status</a>
          </nav>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="app-architecture">
            <h3 id="app-architecture">App architecture</h3>
            <p>A native iPadOS application with local Smart Inks, artworks, biographies, education, relationship history and provenance. Optional online operations travel through Digital Ink’s Cloudflare service to OpenAI.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="smart-ink-model">
            <h3 id="smart-ink-model">Smart Ink data &amp; memory</h3>
            <p>A selected image becomes finite material with a representative colour, identity and recorded origin. Distinct memory layers keep source evidence, personal testimony, learned knowledge and machine invention from quietly becoming the same thing.</p>
            <dl class="memory-record">
              {memoryLayers.map((layer) => (<div><dt>{layer.name}</dt><dd>{layer.text}</dd></div>))}
            </dl>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="ink-rules">
            <h3 id="ink-rules">Scarcity / Ink rules</h3>
            <p>Painting spends finite Ink; erasing can return material. Placed pixels remain connected to their source Ink. Colour, use and recorded history belong to the material rather than an unlimited colour picker.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="ink-education">
            <h3 id="ink-education">Education</h3>
            <p>Selected texts and PDFs can add learned concepts to an Ink. These acquired concepts remain distinct from its original visual description and from personal notes. Education contributes to a developing biography; it is not a claim of consciousness.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="ink-relationships">
            <h3 id="ink-relationships">Relationships &amp; InkHome</h3>
            <p>Version 3.0 introduced relationship history and a spatial InkHome. Smart Inks occupy a projected 3D environment, form routes and reveal connections. A relationship can become active, dormant or historical while retaining its earlier states, explanation and witnesses.</p>
            <aside class="relationship-map" aria-label="Inkhome relationship map">
          <p>INKHOME · LOCAL RELATIONSHIP FIELD</p>
          <div class="relationship-field" aria-hidden="true">
            <i class="relationship-line relationship-line--active"></i>
            <i class="relationship-line relationship-line--dormant"></i>
            <i class="relationship-line relationship-line--historical"></i>
            <span class="relationship-node relationship-node--ink-a"
              >INK 031</span
            >
            <span class="relationship-node relationship-node--ink-b"
              >INK 088</span
            >
            <span class="relationship-node relationship-node--artwork"
              >ARTWORK</span
            >
            <span class="relationship-node relationship-node--artist"
              >ARTIST ID</span
            >
          </div>
          <ul class="relationship-key">
            <li><i class="is-active"></i>ACTIVE</li>
            <li><i class="is-dormant"></i>DORMANT</li>
            <li><i class="is-historical"></i>HISTORICAL</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="relationship-map-note">
            Positions, routes and connections turn the archive into a small
            inhabited system—not only a collection of files.
          </p>
        </aside>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="ink-provenance">
            <h3 id="ink-provenance">Provenance</h3>
            <p>Artworks connect to the artist, Smart Inks and histories involved in making them. New artworks can carry stronger cryptographic verification. Older Smart Inks remain Heritage Ink: incomplete, truthful and never silently upgraded with evidence they did not originally have.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="ink-privacy">
            <h3 id="ink-privacy">Privacy / AI &amp; model information</h3>
            <div class="record-boundaries">
              <article><h4>On device · Apple Vision</h4><p>Apple-provided visual recognition can analyse selected images without credits or an internet connection. Its interpretations may be limited or inaccurate. Local Ink information stays on the device unless you choose a feature that shares selected information.</p></article>
              <article><h4>Optional online · OpenAI</h4><p>With explicit permission for the operation, selected information travels through Cloudflare to OpenAI. Online Assist costs 1 credit; AI Memory Reconstruction costs 10. Purchasing credits is never treated as consent.</p><p>Reconstruction generates a new image. It is an invention, never presented as recovery of the source photograph.</p></article>
            </div>
            <a class="ink-text-link" href="/privacy/">Read the complete Digital Ink Privacy Policy ↗</a>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="voice-research">
            <p class="ink-development-status">IN DEVELOPMENT · FUTURE FEATURE</p>
            <h3 id="voice-research">Canvas Voice research</h3>
            <p>The planned conversation is limited to five words in and five words back. A temporary group of Inks would be able to contribute meaningful signals or remain quiet.</p>
            <p>The design calls for only temporary, privacy-safe concepts to reach the language model. The encounter would not be added to Ink memory after the conversation ends. These describe the intended design of a feature still in development.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="archive-title">
            <p class="ink-kicker">PROPOSED FUTURE RESEARCH EDITION</p>
            <h3 id="archive-title">The 116-Pixel Archive</h3>
            <p>A proposed controlled archive in which 116 photographs become 116 Smart Inks. Version 3.0 supplies relationship history and spatial InkHome; the research edition would give that working foundation a deliberately governed population.</p>
            <p><strong>This edition does not yet exist.</strong> The residency process would discover its rules before a dedicated edition is built.</p>
            <ol class="archive-process">
              <li><h4>Define the archive protocol</h4><p>Source and consent, memory rights, affinity, exchange, forgetting, intervention and what the audience may see.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Run a controlled prototype</h4><p>Twelve photographs test whether the rules create meaningful differences before scale hardens them into software.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Build the dedicated edition</h4><p>Only after observation: 116 Smart Inks with event history, replay, backup and exhibition-safe operation.</p></li>
            </ol>
            <blockquote>What might 116 finite witnesses remember together — without any of them being given a past they never had?</blockquote>
            <p>A dedicated <code>/116</code> process page is planned when the research has a process to show.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="record-section" aria-labelledby="development-status">
            <h3 id="development-status">Current development status</h3>
            <p>Digital Ink is available on the App Store. Version 3.0, released 20 September 2026, includes relationships, InkHome and Artist Identity. Canvas Voice is in development for a future version; the 116-Pixel Archive is a proposed research edition.</p>
            <p>The film and authored interface studies on this page belong to the project’s development history. Their recorded interface states are part of that history.</p>
            <nav class="ink-actions" aria-label="App and policy links"><a class="ink-button" href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/digital-ink/id6757157376" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">App Store ↗</a><a class="ink-button" href="/privacy/">Privacy policy</a><a class="ink-button" href="mailto:hello@q888.space">Contact the artist</a></nav>
          </section>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="ink-footer">
    <div class="ink-wrap">
      <p>Digital Ink<br /><span>An ongoing artwork by Aleksandrs Sestakovs / Q888</span></p>
      <nav aria-label="Digital Ink links"><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/digital-ink/id6757157376" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">App Store ↗</a><a href="/privacy/">Privacy policy</a><a href="mailto:hello@q888.space">Contact</a></nav>
    </div>
  </footer>
</main>

